// Basic's environment: the value of a variable of the environment the
// program was started with, through kernel32's GetEnvironmentVariableW on
// Windows and C's getenv on Linux (libc.rig), a command run through the
// system's shell, through kernel32's CreateProcessW on Windows and C's
// system on Linux, a command run with its standard output captured, the
// same way through a pipe on Windows and through popen on Linux, and the
// command line the program was started with, which Preload saves before
// main's first line.
//
// A variable's name and a command go to the system as a copy, in UTF-16
// and ended by a zero unit on Windows (to_wide_string, wide.rig), and
// ended by a zero on Linux (to_c_string). The system reads each up to its
// first zero, so a name or a command with a zero byte in it would reach it
// cut short, another name or another command, and get_environment,
// run_command and run_command_with_output refuse it without calling the
// system. The copies come from the allocator each procedure is given, the
// context's unless another is, and go back to it before the procedure
// returns, once the system is done with them.
//
// The systems differ in what they are handed, and Basic doesn't hide it:
// Windows' variable names are case-insensitive, so "Path" finds PATH
// there, and Linux's aren't; a command runs in cmd.exe on Windows and in
// sh on Linux, with each one's syntax, cmd's echo ending its line with
// \r\n and sh's with \n; and its status is the command's exit code on
// Windows, and a wait status on Linux (run_command).
//
// A captured output is the bytes the command wrote to its standard output,
// as it wrote them: neither system's pipes have a text mode, so cmd's \r\n
// stays \r\n, as Basic's files keep their bytes (file.rig). Only standard
// output is captured: the command's standard error and standard input are
// the program's own, so its errors still reach the program's standard
// error (a command can send them into the pipe too with 2>&1).
//
// A variable's name and value, and a command, are UTF-8 on both systems.
// Linux's C library takes and gives them as bytes, so UTF-8 stays UTF-8.
// Windows' wide calls take the name and the command and give the value in
// UTF-16, converted from and to UTF-8 (wide.rig), where C's getenv, system
// and _popen would read and give them in the system's ANSI code page, so
// that one with a character outside it, 日本 in code page 1252, would mean
// something else there; a name or a command that isn't valid UTF-8, which
// would have to change to be converted, is refused there. What a command
// writes is its own, in whatever encoding it chooses: on Windows cmd's
// echo writes its text in the console's code page, not in UTF-8, where
// its type writes a file's bytes as they are.
#load "libc.rig";
#load "string.rig";
#load "allocators.rig";
#load "memory.rig";
#load "builder.rig";
#load "wide.rig";

// A copy of the value of the environment's variable name, in memory from
// allocator, the context's unless given, which the caller gives back with
// free(value.data, allocator), and true. On Linux C's getenv gives a
// pointer to its own copy, which a later call may change, so the value is
// copied out at once, as its bytes, its zero not included; on Windows
// GetEnvironmentVariableW writes it, in UTF-16, into a buffer Basic gives
// (below), and it is converted to UTF-8 from there. On Windows an unpaired
// surrogate in the value, which Windows allows, becomes U+FFFD. A variable
// set to "" is the zeroed string (count 0, data null), which allocates
// nothing, and true.
//
// It is the zeroed string and false, with nothing left allocated, when the
// variable isn't set, when the allocator has no memory for the name's copy
// or the value's, and for a name the system would read wrong: an empty
// name, one with '=', which ends a name in the environment (NAME=value),
// one with a zero byte, which the system would read as a shorter name, and
// on Windows one that isn't valid UTF-8, which would have to change to be
// converted and so would name another variable.
//
// On Windows GetEnvironmentVariableW is asked first for the value's size
// in UTF-16 units, its zero counted: 0 when the variable isn't set, and 1
// when it is set to "". The value is then read into a buffer of that size
// on the heap, given back before the call returns, as the file
// procedures' copies of a path are (file.rig says why), and converted into
// one allocation from allocator. A value another thread changed between
// the two calls, so that the second doesn't give one unit fewer than the
// size, is false, as is no memory for the buffer.
//
// The name's copy and the value's are asked for, and the name's copy given
// back, with loc, the place of the call unless given (#caller_location,
// spec 7.2), so an allocator that records where it is asked from sees the
// caller's line (allocators.rig).
get_environment :: (name: string, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location) -> string, bool {
    empty : string;
    if name.count == 0 || find_byte(name, "="[0]) >= 0 || find_byte(name, 0) >= 0 { return empty, false; }
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        // The name's copy is given back as soon as the system has read it,
        // before the value's is asked for, as on Linux, so an allocator
        // sees the same calls in the same order on both systems.
        wide_name := to_wide_string(name, allocator, loc);
        if wide_name == null { return empty, false; }
        size := c_get_environment_variable_w(wide_name, null, 0);
        if size <= 1 {
            free(wide_name, allocator, loc);
            return empty, size == 1;
        }
        units := cast(*u16) alloc(cast(int) size * 2, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
        if units == null {
            free(wide_name, allocator, loc);
            return empty, false;
        }
        defer free(units, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
        got := c_get_environment_variable_w(wide_name, units, size);
        free(wide_name, allocator, loc);
        if got != size - 1 { return empty, false; }
        value, converted := from_wide_string(units, cast(int) got, allocator, loc);
        return value, converted;
    } else {
        c_name := to_c_string(name, allocator, loc);
        if c_name == null { return empty, false; }
        c_value := c_getenv(c_name);
        free(c_name, allocator, loc);
        if c_value == null { return empty, false; }

        // C's string ends at its first zero, which is how its length is
        // found.
        count := 0;
        while c_value[count] != 0 { count += 1; }
        if count == 0 { return empty, true; }
        data := cast(*u8) alloc(count, allocator, loc);
        if data == null { return empty, false; }
        copy_memory(data, c_value, count);

        value : string;
        value.data = data;
        value.count = count;
        return value, true;
    }
}

// Runs command through the system's shell, cmd.exe on Windows and sh on
// Linux, waits for it to end, and gives its status. On Windows that is
// the command's exit code, as C's system gives it: "exit 3" gives 3, a
// command cmd.exe can't find gives 1, and "exit -1" gives -1, the code
// 0xFFFFFFFF read as a signed 32-bit number (run_through_shell). On Linux
// it is C's system's wait status: the exit code times 256 when the
// command ended by itself ("exit 3" gives 768), and 32512 (127 times 256)
// when sh can't find the command. Either way 0 means it succeeded. An
// empty command, "" or the zeroed string, runs the shell with nothing to
// do, which gives 0: on Linux it reaches C as a lone zero (to_c_string),
// never as the null that asks whether there is a shell.
//
// It is -1, and nothing runs, for a command with a zero byte, which the
// system would read as a shorter command, on Windows for one that isn't
// valid UTF-8, and when allocator, the context's unless given, has no
// memory for the command's copy. -1 is also what it gives when the shell
// can't be started, and on Windows what a command that exits with -1
// gives, so -1 alone doesn't say the command never ran.
//
// The program's output may still sit in C's buffer for stdout, where
// Basic's print puts it, while the command writes to the same place
// straight away; so every C stream is flushed first, and what the program
// printed before the command comes out before what the command prints.
//
// The command's copy is asked for and given back with loc, the place of
// the call unless given, as get_environment's are.
run_command :: (command: string, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location) -> int {
    if find_byte(command, 0) >= 0 { return -1; }
    // The command's copy for the system: on Windows the whole command
    // line, in UTF-16 (shell_command_line).
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        c_command := shell_command_line(command, allocator, loc);
    } else {
        c_command := to_c_string(command, allocator, loc);
    }
    if c_command == null { return -1; }
    c_fflush(null);
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        status := run_through_shell(c_command, null);
    } else {
        status := c_system(c_command);
    }
    free(c_command, allocator, loc);
    return status;
}

// Runs command through the system's shell, as run_command does, waits for
// it to end, and gives what it wrote to its standard output, in memory
// from allocator, the context's unless given, which the caller gives back
// with free(output.data, allocator), and its status, as run_command gives
// it: the exit code on Windows, a wait status on Linux, 0 for success on
// both. The output is its bytes as the command wrote them, cmd's \r\n
// included (the header says why). A command that writes nothing gives the
// zeroed string (count 0, data null), which allocates nothing.
//
// Only standard output is captured: what the command writes to standard
// error goes to the program's, as run_command's does, and it reads the
// program's standard input. What the program printed before it is flushed
// first, as run_command flushes it, so it comes out before anything the
// command writes to standard error.
//
// It is the zeroed string and -1, and nothing runs, for every command
// run_command refuses, as its -1 is, and when allocator has no memory for
// the command's copy; and the zeroed string and -1 when the shell can't be
// started. When allocator has no memory for the output, the command has
// run all the same, so its output is lost but its status is still given,
// with the zeroed string.
//
// The output is read from the pipe, 4096 bytes at a time into a buffer on
// the stack, until a read gives nothing more: with ReadFile on Windows
// (run_through_shell), and from the pipe's file descriptor with read on
// Linux. It is gathered in a builder on the heap, whose memory is given
// back before it returns; then it is copied into one allocation of its
// exact size from allocator. The heap is used for the gathering rather
// than allocator, which may be an arena's, where a builder's growing would
// leave each smaller copy behind. Like every builder of Basic's, the
// heap's stops the program when it has no memory left (builder.rig).
//
// The command's copy and the output are asked for, and the copy given
// back, with loc, the place of the call unless given, as get_environment's
// are. The copy is given back as the call returns, after the output's
// memory is asked for, on both systems, so an allocator sees the same
// calls in the same order on both.
run_command_with_output :: (command: string, allocator: Allocator = context.allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location = #caller_location) -> string, int {
    empty : string;
    if find_byte(command, 0) >= 0 { return empty, -1; }
    // The command's copy for the system: on Windows the whole command
    // line, in UTF-16 (shell_command_line).
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        c_command := shell_command_line(command, allocator, loc);
    } else {
        c_command := to_c_string(command, allocator, loc);
    }
    if c_command == null { return empty, -1; }
    defer free(c_command, allocator, loc);

    c_fflush(null);
    gathered : String_Builder;
    builder_init(*gathered, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
    defer builder_free(*gathered);
    #if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
        status := run_through_shell(c_command, *gathered);
    } else {
        // glibc refuses "rb", and its pipes are binary anyway.
        pipe := c_popen(c_command, "r");
        if pipe == null { return empty, -1; }

        descriptor := c_fileno(pipe);
        buffer : [4096]u8;
        while true {
            got := cast(int) c_read(descriptor, *buffer[0], 4096);
            // A read a signal stopped before it read anything is made
            // again: it isn't the end.
            if got < 0 && c_errno_location().* == C_EINTR { continue; }
            if got <= 0 { break; }
            piece : string;
            piece.data = *buffer[0];
            piece.count = got;
            append_string(*gathered, piece);
        }
        status := cast(int) c_pclose(pipe);
    }

    if gathered.count == 0 { return empty, status; }
    data := cast(*u8) alloc(gathered.count, allocator, loc);
    if data == null { return empty, status; }
    copy_memory(data, gathered.data, gathered.count);

    output : string;
    output.data = data;
    output.count = gathered.count;
    return output, status;
}

#if OS == OS_WINDOWS {
    // The command line Windows runs command with, for run_command and
    // run_command_with_output: "SHELL" /c COMMAND, in UTF-16 with a zero
    // unit after it, in one allocation from allocator at loc, which the
    // caller gives back with free(line, allocator, loc) once the shell has
    // ended, as Linux's C copy is given back. The shell is the one
    // %COMSPEC% names, cmd.exe's path, or cmd.exe when it is unset or
    // empty, which CreateProcessW looks for where it looks for any program
    // named without a path: the program's own directory first, then the
    // current one, the system's and PATH (C's system tries the current
    // directory and PATH); a %COMSPEC% naming a shell that isn't there
    // gives -1 (run_through_shell). The shell's path is in quotes, so that
    // one with a space in it is one word; cmd.exe takes what follows /c as
    // the command, as it does from system, so a command means what it
    // meant there.
    //
    // It is null, with nothing allocated, when the command line isn't valid
    // UTF-8 or allocator has no memory for it. The line is built in a
    // builder on the heap, and %COMSPEC% looked up on the heap (its name's
    // UTF-16 copy, get_environment's buffer and its value), all given back
    // before it returns.
    shell_command_line :: (command: string, allocator: Allocator, loc: Source_Code_Location) -> *u16 {
        shell, found := get_environment("COMSPEC", HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
        defer free(shell.data, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
        line : String_Builder;
        builder_init(*line, HEAP_ALLOCATOR);
        defer builder_free(*line);
        append_string(*line, "\"");
        if found && shell.count > 0 {
            append_string(*line, shell);
        } else {
            append_string(*line, "cmd.exe");
        }
        append_string(*line, "\" /c ");
        append_string(*line, command);
        text : string;
        text.data = line.data;
        text.count = line.count;
        return to_wide_string(text, allocator, loc);
    }

    // Windows' way to run a command line of shell_command_line's, for
    // run_command, with captured null, and run_command_with_output, with
    // the builder it gathers the output in: the shell is started by
    // CreateProcessW with wide_line as its command line.
    //
    // The shell starts with the program's own standard input, output and
    // error, so what it writes goes where the program's own output goes,
    // after what the caller flushed. When captured isn't null, its
    // standard output is instead the write end of a pipe, which only the
    // shell holds once it has started: the program reads the other end,
    // appending each piece to captured, until the read gives nothing, when
    // the shell, and every command it started, has ended and closed it.
    // Then it waits for the shell to end and gives its exit code as a
    // signed 32-bit number, as system gives it, so "exit -1" is -1, not
    // 4294967295.
    //
    // It is -1, with nothing run, when the pipe can't be made or the shell
    // started.
    run_through_shell :: (wide_line: *u16, captured: *String_Builder) -> int {
        startup : C_Startup_Info;
        startup.size = size_of(C_Startup_Info);
        startup.flags = C_STARTF_USESTDHANDLES;
        startup.standard_input = c_get_std_handle(C_STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
        startup.standard_output = c_get_std_handle(C_STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        startup.standard_error = c_get_std_handle(C_STD_ERROR_HANDLE);

        // Both of the pipe's ends are made inheritable, and then the
        // program's own, the read end, made not: the shell inherits only
        // the write end, which it is given as its standard output.
        read_end : *void = null;
        write_end : *void = null;
        if captured != null {
            security : C_Security_Attributes;
            security.length = size_of(C_Security_Attributes);
            security.inherit = 1;
            if c_create_pipe(*read_end, *write_end, *security, 0) == 0 { return -1; }
            if c_set_handle_information(read_end, C_HANDLE_FLAG_INHERIT, 0) == 0 {
                c_close_handle(read_end);
                c_close_handle(write_end);
                return -1;
            }
            startup.standard_output = write_end;
        }

        information : C_Process_Information;
        started := c_create_process_w(null, wide_line, null, null, 1, 0, null, null, *startup, *information);
        if captured != null {
            // The program's copy of the write end is closed, started or
            // not, so that once the shell has started its copy is the only
            // one, and a read gives nothing when the shell has closed it.
            c_close_handle(write_end);
            if started == 0 { c_close_handle(read_end); }
        }
        if started == 0 { return -1; }

        if captured != null {
            buffer : [4096]u8;
            while true {
                // A read gives 0 bytes, or fails, once no write end is
                // left open, and the pipe is empty.
                got : u32 = 0;
                if c_read_file(read_end, *buffer[0], 4096, *got, null) == 0 || got == 0 { break; }
                piece : string;
                piece.data = *buffer[0];
                piece.count = cast(int) got;
                append_string(captured, piece);
            }
            c_close_handle(read_end);
        }

        c_wait_for_single_object(information.process, C_INFINITE);
        code : u32 = 0;
        got_code := c_get_exit_code_process(information.process, *code);
        c_close_handle(information.thread);
        c_close_handle(information.process);
        if got_code == 0 { return -1; }
        return cast(int) cast,trunc(s32) code;
    }
}

// The program's command-line arguments (spec 7.4): the name it was started
// by first, then each argument. On Windows they are UTF-8: a compiled
// program's are converted by its start-up from the C runtime's wide ones,
// an interpreted one's by interpret mode from the wide command line, so,
// like a variable's name and value above, they don't depend on the ANSI
// code page; on Linux they are the bytes the system gave, as they are.
// The view is empty, not even a name, when the program was started with
// no argv[0] (argc 0, which Linux before 5.18 allowed), so check its count
// before reading the name. Each string is followed by a zero byte its
// count doesn't include, so its data can go to C as it is. main's
// prologue saves them, through Preload's __save_command_line, before
// main's first line, and this gives the view it saved: the same one every
// call, its elements and their bytes the program's for the whole run,
// never freed, and as changeable as any global's. A #run sees the empty
// view, not even a name: no startup runs while compiling (spec 7.8), and
// the compiler's own arguments aren't the program's.
get_command_line_arguments :: () -> []string {
    return __command_line_arguments;
}
